          {
            "creation_time": "2025-09-05T09:08:11Z",
            "creator": "gabembecker",
            "bug_id": 18944,
            "text": "Created attachment 3540\npatch to add pre-parsing filter step to aspell() with motivating reg test\n\nKurt proposed a pair of issues related to making spell checking more robust. This is our attempt to address the first, as paraphrased by Heather Turner in (https://github.com/r-devel/r-dev-day/issues/85):\n\n>For files in LaTeX, both Aspell and Hunspell provide filters; only the former is extensible via lists of additional macros (LaTeX commands) to be handled. Neither provides filtering of non-text inside \\verb or filtering of environments (including verbatim ones). In 2010, Duncan Murdoch added a LaTeX parser to the tools package and has recently made considerable enhancements to this parser, as well as implementing a new one, currently in CRAN package parseLatex. Package utils provides experimental code for R-based LaTeX filtering using these parsers, which allows specifying additional filter rules for both commands and environments, and specifying (additional) verbatim commands and environments. However, parsers fail when they cannot parse, in which case the filtering does not work. It would be nice to explore how the C++ codes for LaTeX filtering in Aspell or Hunspell handle things, and either try to implement extensible filters which do not attempt full parsing (in R or C) using the ideas in these codes, or maybe teach the parser to optionally be more graceful when only filtering is desired.\n\nOur proposed approach is to add a *pre-parsing* filter step to the `aspell()` R function which, when specified, can be a filter which is explicitly expected to operate only on raw text and not assume it's able to parse the input. The result of this filter is then passed to the traditional filters, which can assume the ability to parse. In principle this could be useful for any format, though I'm only aware of it being useful for LaTex because of imperfections in the R LaTeX parsers that are unable to handle urls with `%` in them within certain TeX commands out of the box. We've added a regression test with an Rnw file that trips errors in `R 4.5.1` using the settings for R vignettes, but passes with the patch.\n\nThis work is joint with Heather, proposed patch attached.",
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "attachment_id": 3540,
            "tags": [],
            "id": 102198,
            "time": "2025-09-05T09:08:11Z",
            "is_private": false
          },
          {
